## 6.5 One PDF May Contain ALL Modules

This is a key requirement.

The HOD may upload one PDF that includes questions from multiple modules.

The questions may appear in ascending module order or in a mixed order.

The system must identify the correct module for each question and group it accordingly.

### Module detection methods

Use the following evidence, in priority order:

1. Explicit question-level labels such as M1, M2, M3, M4.
2. Module headings such as MODULE 1, MODULE 2.
3. Section headings that apply to subsequent questions.
4. Page-level module labels.
5. Available subject module names/topics where sufficient evidence exists.

### Examples

Document A:

Module 1:
Q1 ...
Q2 ...
Q3 ...

Module 2:
Q4 ...
Q5 ...
Q6 ...

The converter must extract and group questions under Modules 1 and 2.

Document B:

Q1 [M2] ...
Q2 [M1] ...
Q3 [M3] ...
Q4 [M1] ...
Q5 [M2] ...

The converter must assign each question using its own module label, even though the questions are mixed.

Document C:

M1 — Question 1  
M2 — Question 2  
M1 — Question 3  
M3 — Question 4

The converter must group the questions correctly.

### Module context rules

When a module heading applies to subsequent questions, carry that module context until another valid module heading appears.

When individual questions explicitly state M1, M2, etc., use the individual label.

Do not let the previous module heading override an explicit question-level module label.

If the PDF clearly identifies different modules, separate them automatically.

If a question's module cannot be confidently identified:
- Mark it as Needs Module Review.
- Allow the HOD to choose its correct module manually.
- Do not silently assign an incorrect module.
- Do not discard the question.

Use explicit document labels as the primary classification evidence.

Use configured subject module topics only as supporting classification evidence.

Do not call external AI services to classify modules.

Do not invent module names or syllabus topics.

