# PART 8 — FRONTEND-ONLY CONVERSION MUST REMAIN WORKING AFTER BACKEND INTEGRATION

This requirement needs special attention.

The document conversion engines must run locally in the browser, independently of the Java backend.

The workflow must remain:

Original PDF/Image → Browser Extraction/OCR → Browser-side Module/Record Classification → Editable Preview → Validation → Browser-side Excel Generation → Download.

No backend request is required for these conversion steps.

### When the Java backend is later connected

The conversion engine must remain unchanged and continue running in the browser.

Only the normal manual Excel upload/save step should connect to the backend.

For example:

Student:

Local PDF conversion → Download standard Excel → Manual Excel upload → Backend validation and database saving.

Questions:

Local multi-module PDF conversion → Download separate module-specific Excel files → Manual Excel upload → Backend validation and question-bank saving.

Do not:
- Redirect original PDF uploads to Java conversion endpoints.
- Replace working browser conversion with a backend-only conversion API.
- Disable browser conversion after authentication or API integration is added.
- Make converter functionality dependent on PostgreSQL availability.
- Display Backend Unavailable for the local conversion operation.
- Use remote AI or cloud OCR.

The Java backend is responsible for persistent storage of manually uploaded, validated Excel records, not document conversion.

If the manual Excel save endpoint is unavailable, show an honest backend-not-connected error while preserving the downloaded Excel and local conversion result.

